Candidiasis
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Beefy-red erythema, Satellite papules/pustules, Intertriginous distribution

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-010
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe burning and intense pruritus in intertriginous areas resulting in skin breakdown and extreme discomfort.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a diabetic patient suffering from acute, intolerable burning, intense itching, and weeping skin in the groin and abdominal folds, preventing them from dressing or walking. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for secondary bacterial cellulitis, manage the acute pain/pruritus, and initiate potent topical antifungal therapy, avoiding further skin maceration and systemic deterioration before morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My skin is raw and on fire. It burns so badly I can't even put clothes on or walk across the room."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 5-day history of worsening rash in the bilateral inguinal creases and beneath the abdominal panniculus. It began as a mild itch but has rapidly progressed to intense pruritus and a severe burning sensation over the last 48 hours. The patient describes the areas as being bright red and "weeping." They note exacerbation following a recent 10-day course of broad-spectrum antibiotics (Amoxicillin-Clavulanate) for a respiratory infection. The patient has type 2 diabetes mellitus, which has been poorly controlled recently. The severe pain and inability to ambulate prompted the urgent call.
Functional Status: Temporarily bed-bound due to severe pain with friction during ambulation.
Social/Safety Concerns: Poorly controlled diabetes increases risk of severe secondary infection; immobility.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 | HR: 92 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 110 Kg
General: Visibly uncomfortable, lying supine with legs abducted to prevent skin-on-skin contact.
Physical Exam: Examination of the bilateral inguinal folds and subpannicular region reveals confluent, bright beefy-red erythematous patches. In the depths of the skin folds, there is notable maceration and superficial fissuring with a malodorous, serous exudate. Extending beyond the main, sharply defined borders of the erythema are numerous characteristic satellite papules and pustules. There is no spreading induration, localized heat suggesting deep cellulitis, or regional lymphadenopathy. Examination of the oropharynx reveals no thick white plaques.
Environmental Observations: Warm, humid room.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient recently completed Amoxicillin-Clavulanate. On Metformin 1000mg BD.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Cutaneous Candidiasis (Intertrigo) - Status: Acute Exacerbation
Differentials:

Tinea Cruris - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Tinea typically spares the scrotum and deep creases, lacks satellite pustules, and presents with an active scaly border with central clearing, rather than uniform beefy erythema.

Bacterial Intertrigo / Erythrasma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of satellite pustules and severe acute inflammation, contrasting with the mild, brown-red, finely scaling appearance of erythrasma.

Inverse Psoriasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The acute, painful, weeping nature of the rash and presence of satellite pustules. Inverse psoriasis is typically chronic, smooth, and sharply demarcated without pustules.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to diabetes and immobility)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe, debilitating intertriginous candidiasis, classic in its presentation of beefy-red erythema, maceration, and satellite pustules. The recent antibiotic use and poorly controlled diabetes are prime exacerbating factors. Triage correctly identified the severe burning pain, skin breakdown, and loss of mobility as an acute crisis requiring prompt attention. Urgent after-hours assessment was necessary to differentiate this from an acute bacterial intertriginous cellulitis, provide immediate barrier protection to the macerated skin, and prescribe combined antifungal/anti-inflammatory topical treatments to rapidly arrest the intense suffering and prevent deeper ulceration. Delaying care would have resulted in intolerable pain and inevitable secondary bacterial infection.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical Clotrimazole 1% / Hydrocortisone 1% cream applied BD to reduce fungal load and acute inflammation. Recommended application of a zinc oxide barrier paste over the treated areas to prevent friction and maceration.
Positive Behavioral Change: By thoroughly gently patting your skin folds completely dry after showering and using the hair dryer on a cool setting, you will eliminate the moisture that yeast needs to survive, drastically speeding up your healing. By applying the prescribed cream strictly twice a day, you will quickly calm the severe burning and kill the fungus, allowing you to walk comfortably again within 48 hours.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: GP review in 3 days to assess clinical response, optimize diabetic control, and ensure no secondary bacterial infection has developed.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the redness spreads rapidly beyond the groin, if the fluid becomes thick yellow pus, or if you develop a fever over 38.0C, you must seek immediate medical attention via the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Fungal infections thrive in warm, moist environments; strict adherence to skin care is required alongside the medication.
Clinical Course: The severe burning and itching will significantly improve within 24-48 hours of starting the cream. Complete clearance of the rash will take 1-2 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Monitor for spreading redness, worsening pain, or fever, which could indicate a bacterial infection taking over the broken skin.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The recent antibiotics killed your good skin bacteria, allowing a natural yeast to overgrow in the damp skin folds. The combination cream kills the yeast and stops the agonizing allergic inflammation.
Actionable Steps: Avoid tight clothing. Do not vigorously rub the area dry; pat it gently to avoid further tearing the fragile skin.

